::ALG2_U05_UA_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y=x+1 and y=5. {
= x=4#Correct. 5=x+1 gives x=4.
~ x=5#This is the y-value.
~ x=1#This is the intercept.
~ x=6#This adds instead of subtracting.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y>2x and y<6? {
~ (3,7)#7 is not less than 6.
~ (2,3)#3 is not greater than 4.
~ (4,9)#9 is not less than 6.
= (1,4)#Correct. 4>2 and 4<6.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>A nonlinear system has intersections at (-2,4) and (3,9). What are the solutions? {
~ x=1 only#This is not listed as an intersection.
~ No solution#Two intersections are given.
= x=-2 and x=3#Correct. System solutions are the intersection x-values with their points.
~ x=4 and x=9#These are y-values only.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>How does y=(x+2)<sup>2</sup> transform y=x<sup>2</sup>? {
~ Down 2#No outside -2 appears.
= Left 2#Correct. x+2 shifts left 2.
~ Right 2#The inside sign is opposite.
~ Up 2#No outside +2 appears.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+2 and g(x)=3x, what is f(g(x))? {
= 3x+2#Correct. Substitute 3x into f.
~ 3x<sup>2</sup>+2#This multiplies incorrectly.
~ 4x+2#This adds functions.
~ 3x-2#The constant sign is wrong.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=x+8. {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x+8#This repeats the original.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=8x#This does not undo addition.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(x+8)#Inverse notation is not reciprocal.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x-8#Correct. Subtract 8 to undo adding 8.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>What does solving f(x)=g(x) find? {
~ Only domain restrictions#Restrictions are allowed inputs.
~ Only maximum values#Maxima are different features.
= Intersections#Correct. Equal outputs at the same input are intersections.
~ Only y-intercepts#That checks x=0 only.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y=2x and y=10. {
~ x=20#This multiplies 10 by 2 instead of solving 10=2x by division.
= x=5#Correct. 10=2x gives x=5.
~ x=10#This is the y-value from the horizontal equation, not the x-solution.
~ x=2#This is the coefficient.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which region satisfies y<=x+1? {
= On or below the line#Correct. <= includes the boundary and values below.
~ Above the line only#That would be >.
~ Only the y-axis#The solution is a half-plane.
~ No points#There are many solutions.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>If a system solution is (-1,3), what does it mean? {
~ Only the first equation is true#A system solution satisfies both.
~ Only y=0#The y-value is 3.
~ The graphs never meet#A solution is a meeting point.
= Both equations are true at (-1,3)#Correct. The point satisfies both equations.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>How does y=x<sup>2</sup>+5 transform y=x<sup>2</sup>? {
~ Left 5#Horizontal shifts are inside the input.
~ Right 5#Horizontal shifts are inside the input.
= Up 5#Correct. Outside +5 shifts the graph up.
~ Down 5#The sign is positive.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=2x and g(x)=x-1, what is g(f(4))? {
~ 10#This adds instead of subtracting.
= 7#Correct. f(4)=8 and g(8)=7.
~ 8#This stops after f(4).
~ 6#This applies g to 4 first then doubles.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=3x. {
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x/3#Correct. Divide by 3 to undo multiplying by 3.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=3x#This repeats the original.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x-3#This undoes addition.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(3x)#This is reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>What is the average rate of change from (1,4) to (5,12)? {
~ 8#This is the output change.
~ 4#This is the input change.
~ 16#This adds coordinates.
= 2#Correct. (12-4)/(5-1)=2.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y=x-3 and y=0. {
~ x=0#This uses the given y-value as if it were the x-solution.
~ x=1#This does not satisfy the equation.
= x=3#Correct. x-3=0.
~ x=-3#This reverses the sign; x-3=0 means x must equal 3.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y>=x<sup>2</sup> for x=2? {
~ (2,-1)#-1 is less than 4.
= (2,5)#Correct. 5>=4.
~ (2,3)#3 is less than 4.
~ (2,0)#0 is less than 4.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>A feasible solution must do what? {
= Satisfy all constraints#Correct. Feasible means all restrictions are met.
~ Satisfy one constraint only#All constraints matter.
~ Ignore inequalities#Inequalities define feasibility.
~ Be negative#Sign depends on context.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>How does y=2x<sup>2</sup> compare to y=x<sup>2</sup>? {
~ Shift up 2#No outside addition appears.
~ Shift right 2#No input change appears.
~ Reflection#The coefficient is positive.
= Vertical stretch by 2#Correct. Outputs are doubled.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If (f+g)(x) for f=x and g=5x, what is the result? {
~ 4x#This subtracts.
~ x+5#This treats g as constant.
= 6x#Correct. Add x+5x.
~ 5x<sup>2</sup>#This multiplies.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x/4, what is f<sup>-1</sup>(x)? {
~ 1/(x/4)#This is reciprocal.
= 4x#Correct. Multiply by 4 to undo division.
~ x/4#This repeats the original.
~ x-4#This undoes addition.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Which graph feature occurs where y=0? {
= x-intercept#Correct. x-intercepts have output 0.
~ y-intercept#That occurs where x=0.
~ domain#Domain is input set.
~ range#Range is output set.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 2x+1=9. {
~ x=5#This adds instead of subtracting first.
~ x=8#This stops before dividing.
~ x=10#This adds values instead of isolating x after subtracting 1.
= x=4#Correct. 2x=8, so x=4.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement about f<sup>-1</sup>(x) is correct? {
~ It always equals -f(x)#That is opposite, not inverse.
~ It always has no graph#Inverse functions can be graphed.
= It reverses the input-output relationship#Correct. That is inverse function meaning.
~ It always equals 1/f(x)#That is reciprocal confusion.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>If f(2)=6 and g(2)=9, which is greater at x=2? {
~ Neither exists#Both values are given.
= g#Correct. 9 is greater than 6.
~ f#6 is less than 9.
~ They are equal#The outputs differ.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>For (f/g)(x), what must be excluded? {
= Inputs where g(x)=0#Correct. The denominator cannot be zero.
~ Inputs where f(x)=0#A zero numerator is allowed.
~ All positive inputs#Positive inputs are not automatically excluded.
~ All inputs#Only denominator zeros are excluded.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q26::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q26<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup>=16. {
~ x=4 only#This misses the negative root.
~ x=8#This is not a square root.
~ x=16#This is not a solution.
= x=-4 and x=4#Correct. Both square to 16.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q27::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q27<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation is a reflection over the x-axis? {
~ y=f(x)+1#That shifts up.
~ y=f(x-1)#That shifts right.
= y=-f(x)#Correct. Negating outputs reflects over the x-axis.
~ y=f(-x)#That reflects over the y-axis.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q28::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q28<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+4 and g(x)=x-4, what is f(g(x))? {
~ 2x#This adds functions.
= x#Correct. The operations undo each other.
~ x+8#This adds both shifts.
~ x-8#This subtracts both shifts.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q29::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q29<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>What is the inverse of f(x)=2x-6? {
= (x+6)/2#Correct. Add 6, then divide by 2.
~ (x-6)/2#The sign is wrong.
~ 2x+6#This does not reverse operations.
~ 1/(2x-6)#This is reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q30::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q30<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Which value is not viable for number of items sold? {
~ 0#Selling zero items can be viable.
~ 4#Positive counts can be viable.
~ 12#Positive counts can be viable.
= -2#Correct. A count sold cannot be negative.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q31::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q31<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y=3x and y=x+4. {
~ x=6#This is y at the solution.
~ x=0#This does not satisfy both.
= x=2#Correct. 3x=x+4 gives 2x=4.
~ x=4#This is not the x-solution.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q32::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q32<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y<5 and y>x? {
~ (5,5)#5 is not less than 5.
= (2,4)#Correct. 4<5 and 4>2.
~ (6,4)#4 is not greater than 6.
~ (2,6)#6 is not less than 5.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q33::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q33<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which system type can have two intersections? {
= A line and a parabola#Correct. A line and parabola can intersect twice.
~ Two identical constants only#That description is incomplete.
~ Only parallel lines#Parallel lines do not intersect.
~ Only one horizontal line#One equation alone is not a system.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q34::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q34<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>What does y=f(x)+3 do? {
~ Shift down 3#The sign is positive.
~ Shift left 3#Horizontal changes are inside input.
~ Reflect#No negative factor appears.
= Shift up 3#Correct. Adding outside shifts outputs up.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q35::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q35<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x<sup>2</sup> and g(x)=x+1, what is f(g(x))? {
~ x<sup>2</sup>(x+1)#This multiplies.
~ 2x+1#This is not the composition.
= (x+1)<sup>2</sup>#Correct. Substitute x+1 into f.
~ x<sup>2</sup>+1#This adds after squaring.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q36::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q36<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Which check verifies inverse functions? {
~ f(x)=g(x)#Inverses are not usually identical.
= f(g(x))=x and g(f(x))=x#Correct. Both compositions must return x.
~ f(x)+g(x)=x#Addition is not the inverse check.
~ f(x)g(x)=1#This is reciprocal reasoning.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q37::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q37<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>A table shows outputs increasing by 5 whenever inputs increase by 1. What is the rate? {
= 5#Correct. Output change per one input is 5.
~ 1#This is the input change.
~ 6#This adds changes.
~ 0#The output is changing.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q38::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q38<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.3<br><br>Question:<br>What does a solution to a system of inequalities represent? {
~ Only an intersection point#Inequality systems often have regions.
~ Only a y-intercept#That is one graph feature.
~ A point outside every shaded region#That would not satisfy constraints.
= A point in the overlap of all solution regions#Correct. It must satisfy every inequality.
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q39::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q39<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best explains composition order in f(g(x))? {
~ Add f and g#Composition is not addition.
~ Multiply f and g#Composition is not multiplication.
= Apply g first, then f#Correct. The inner function is evaluated first.
~ Apply f first, then g#That would be g(f(x)).
}

::ALG2_U05_UA_Q40::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_UA_Q40<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>Why check restrictions after solving? {
~ It makes every answer negative#Restrictions depend on the situation.
= Some algebraic solutions may not be allowed#Correct. Restrictions and context can reject candidates.
~ Restrictions never matter#They often matter.
~ It changes correct equations into inequalities#Checking does not change problem type.
}

